# EAS Build archives the project by shallow-cloning it into a temp dir and
# re-materializing every entry there, including symlinks — .agents/skills/*
# are real NTFS symlinks into .claude/skills/* (Claude Code's own skill
# tooling, unrelated to the app), and creating a symlink on Windows without
# Developer Mode enabled fails with EPERM, which aborts the whole upload
# ("Failed to upload the project tarball to EAS Build"). Neither directory
# is ever imported by app code — pure dev tooling — so there's no reason
# for EAS to touch them at all. .easignore (unlike .gitignore) excludes
# files from the build archive even though they're tracked by git, which
# is what actually stops EAS from hitting the symlink.
.agents/
.claude/
